.TH T3V 1 "2026-08-09" "t3v 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
t3v \- generate Tor v3 vanity .onion addresses on NVIDIA GPUs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B t3v
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I PREFIX
.RI [, PREFIX ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B t3v
brute-forces Ed25519 keypairs on every detected CUDA device to find .onion v3
addresses beginning with a chosen prefix. Each match is written to the
destination directory as a Tor
.B hs_ed25519_secret_key
file named after the address it produces.
.PP
Positional prefixes are
.IR required :
the run exits as soon as every one has been found. Give several
comma-separated.
.PP
Prefixes are case-insensitive and may only use the base32 alphabet
.BR a\-z " and " 2\-7 .
The digits
.BR 0 ", " 1 ", " 8 " and " 9
are never valid in an onion address. A prefix longer than 51 characters can
never match and is rejected. Each additional character multiplies the expected
search time by 32.
.PP
Every GPU match is re-derived independently on the CPU before anything is
written, so a key whose address does not actually carry the requested prefix is
discarded rather than saved.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dst " " \fIDIR\fR
Directory to write keys into. Defaults to the current directory, which must
already exist.
.TP
.BR \-\-bonus " " \fIPREFIX\fR[,\fIPREFIX\fR...]
Additional prefixes to search for and save if they turn up, but which never
keep the run alive on their own. Useful for a longer nice-to-have prefix you
will take only if it happens to appear.
.TP
.BR \-\-count " " \fIN\fR
Collect
.I N
matches of each prefix before considering it satisfied. Defaults to 1, and
applies to bonus prefixes as well.
.TP
.BR \-\-algo " " \fIALGO\fR
Search kernel to use.
.RS
.TP
.B incremental
Default. Each thread computes one point and then steps to the next candidate by
point addition, with modular inversions batched. Roughly 200 times faster.
Stores the secret scalar un-clamped \(em see
.B UN-CLAMPED SCALARS
below.
.TP
.B seed
The original reference path: hash a fresh random seed per candidate, which
costs a full scalar multiplication every time. Stores a clamped, SHA-512
expanded scalar.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version.
.SH OUTPUT
On an interactive terminal
.B t3v
draws a live dashboard showing, per prefix,
.B CHANCE
\(em the probability a match has turned up by now \(em and
.BR E[NEXT] ,
the expected wait for the next one. The search draws a fresh random base every
launch and so is memoryless:
.B E[NEXT]
stays constant rather than counting down, because running longer does not bring
the next hit closer. When standard output is not a terminal, plain status lines
are printed every 30 seconds instead.
.PP
Key files are created with mode 0600 and flushed to disk before being reported.
An existing file is never overwritten.
.SH UN-CLAMPED SCALARS
Keys produced by the
.B incremental
kernel store the secret scalar directly, un-clamped, which is what Tor does
with the on-disk key. Any tool that recovers the public key by clamping \(em
including ed25519-dalek's ExpandedSecretKey conversion \(em will silently derive
a
.I different
address from such a file. Tor itself is unaffected. Verify a key independently
with the shipped example:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cargo run --release --example validate_key -- <keyfile>
.EE
.RE
.SH RELATED KEYS
Every candidate within one GPU launch is derived from a single random base, so
two keys taken from the same launch would have secret scalars a small, easily
searched distance apart \(em whoever held one could recover the other.
.B t3v
therefore keeps at most one match per launch and re-seeds before the next, so
every key it writes is independent of every other. Matches discarded for this
reason are counted and reported in the run summary.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B T3V_ITERS
Pin the number of keygen iterations each thread performs per launch and skip
the startup autotune. Intended for benchmarking a specific value.
.TP
.B KERNEL_TARGET_CPU
Build-time only. Minimum compute capability the PTX kernel targets, default
.BR sm_75 .
The driver JIT-compiles PTX to the installed GPU at load, so the default runs
on Turing and newer. Do not raise it above the oldest GPU you intend to use.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All required prefixes were found.
.TP
.B 1
Every GPU worker stopped before the search finished; the underlying errors are
printed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments, or no CUDA device is available.
.SH EXAMPLES
Search for one prefix, writing keys into
.IR mykeys/ :
.PP
.RS
.EX
t3v --dst mykeys/ myprefix
.EE
.RE
.PP
Search for two prefixes at once and take a longer one only if it appears:
.PP
.RS
.EX
t3v --dst mykeys/ shaonsen,paarth --bonus shaonsenllc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Collect three distinct addresses for the same prefix:
.PP
.RS
.EX
t3v --dst mykeys/ --count 3 myprefix
.EE
.RE
.PP
Install a generated key into a Tor hidden service directory:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cp mykeys/myprefix\....onion /var/lib/tor/hidden_service/hs_ed25519_secret_key
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tor (1)
.PP
Project home: https://github.com/sureserverman/tor-v3-vanity
.SH AUTHOR
The incremental search kernel is a GPU port of the approach used by mkp224o
(https://github.com/cathugger/mkp224o).
